From 5aa5ccac4d42bc5ae50bc5f273eabc7f0d2df765 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: AOSP RK3576 Baseline <uhf-platform@localbuild>
Date: Tue, 15 Sep 2026 09:45:18 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 01/10] uhf: add the Phase 2 vendor AIDL HAL
 (rockchip.uhf.aidl)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

New project — unlike the other three repos in this patch series, there is
no pre-existing vendor baseline to snapshot here; this commit is the
addition. Implements UHF-SRS-001 §3.2/§5.3/§10.2 Phase 2, the HAL split
that lets the Phase 1 JNI shortcut in frameworks/base be retired.

Naming and file layout follow this tree own precedent for a custom
Rockchip AIDL service, hardware/rockchip/hwc_proxy_service/, checked
directly rather than assumed:
- aidl_interface module rockchip.uhf.aidl (package rockchip.uhf.aidl),
  not vendor.rockchip.hardware.uhf as UHF-SRS-001 Rev B guessed from
  general Android convention — this tree real custom AIDL HALs use the
  shorter rockchip.<name>.aidl shape instead.
- cc_binary rockchip.uhf-service, vintf_fragments plus a separate
  DEVICE_FRAMEWORK_COMPATIBILITY_MATRIX_FILE fragment (both required —
  hwc_proxy_service ships both for the same reason).
- sepolicy: hal_uhf_default domain (Treble standard hal_x_default shape,
  since this genuinely is a HAL default impl) plus the hal_uhf_service /
  service_contexts pair hwc_proxy_service own comment explains is
  required for an NDK-backend AIDL service — a real gap this research
  closed that UHF-SRS-001 original sepolicy sketch had missed. Runs as
  user root / group system in its rc, matching both hwc_proxy and
  power-aidl-rockchip precedent — restriction is the SELinux domain
  transition (init_daemon_domain), not the Linux uid, and only
  hal_uhf_default may touch a node labelled uhf_device regardless of
  what else also happens to run as root or system.
- uhf_hal.mk inherited from device/rockchip/rk3576/rk3576_u/rk3576_u.mk
  specifically (a separate commit in that repo), not from the shared
  device/rockchip/common/device.mk the way hwc_proxy_service.mk is —
  not every Rockchip board has this reader.

UhfReader.cpp and UhfWireProtocol.h: UhfWireProtocol.h is a deliberate,
close port of com.android.server.uhf.UhfProtocol CRC16/frame/scan/resync
logic (37 passing JUnit tests cover that algorithm on the Java side).
UhfReader.cpp reuses it for transact() multi-frame assembly and the
streaming pump loop, rather than reimplementing frame handling a second
time with its own bugs to find. Status-to-error mapping and payload
decoding stay in Java (UhfHalTransport / UhfSessionArbiter), matching
SRS §3.2 "HAL stays thin" design.

STATUS: none of this has been built or run. No framework or vendor build
was attempted (would need the aidl_interface plus cc_binary toolchain),
and there is no reader attached to this environment regardless. The one
piece of it that inherits real confidence rather than none is
UhfWireProtocol.h, by virtue of being a faithful port of tested code —
everything else here (the termios usage, the AIDL/Binder plumbing, the
sepolicy) is standard-shaped but unverified, exactly like UHF-SRS-001 own
Confirm status on a command code: written from the best evidence
available, not yet exercised against the real thing.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
